WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.066
Mika expected the weekend deposit to equal
hours multiplied by the hourly rate, but the
deposit is smaller.

00:00:06.296 --> 00:00:09.955
The first move is not to accuse anyone or
assume every line is correct.

00:00:10.185 --> 00:00:12.463
Find the pay-period dates and the hours
covered.

00:00:12.693 --> 00:00:19.101
This classroom pay stub is fictional; its
dates, hours, dollar values, and deductions
are invented.

00:00:19.331 --> 00:00:25.012
We are learning how to read and reconcile a
statement, not estimating a real student’s tax
or benefits.

00:00:25.092 --> 00:00:27.837
Gross earnings are the starting amount before
deductions.

00:00:28.067 --> 00:00:33.666
For this fictional hourly example, multiply
12.5 hours by $18 per hour.

00:00:33.896 --> 00:00:37.555
The units cancel: hours times dollars per hour
gives dollars.

00:00:37.785 --> 00:00:45.599
Twelve times eighteen is 216, and half an hour
at eighteen dollars is nine, so gross pay is
$225.

00:00:45.829 --> 00:00:48.176
Compare that result with the printed gross
line.

00:00:48.406 --> 00:00:54.882
It matches here, but the match cannot
establish that the recorded 12.5 hours are the
hours Mika actually worked.

00:00:54.962 --> 00:00:58.894
Now read the labels and determine which lines
are deducted from this fictional cheque.

00:00:59.124 --> 00:01:05.554
Add the three listed amounts: sixteen dollars
plus ten dollars and eighty cents plus three
dollars and sixty cents.

00:01:05.784 --> 00:01:08.166
The total is thirty dollars and forty cents.

00:01:08.396 --> 00:01:15.092
In real payroll, eligibility, amounts,
employer arrangements, and current rules
depend on the situation.

00:01:15.322 --> 00:01:22.207
These invented figures are not current
contribution rates, tax guidance, or evidence
that a real deduction should apply.

00:01:22.287 --> 00:01:25.708
Net pay is gross pay minus the deductions
taken from this payment.

00:01:25.938 --> 00:01:34.475
Subtract the total once: 225 dollars minus 30
dollars and 40 cents equals 194 dollars and 60
cents.

00:01:34.705 --> 00:01:36.286
The printed net amount matches.

00:01:36.516 --> 00:01:40.665
Do not subtract each deduction from the
previous line and accidentally omit one.

00:01:40.895 --> 00:01:50.454
Also check whether the document has a
different payment method, an adjustment, or an
informational employer-paid amount before
assuming that the net line equals the bank
deposit.

00:01:50.534 --> 00:01:52.927
Calculate gross pay and total deductions.

00:01:53.157 --> 00:01:56.167
What does a match establish—and what does it
not prove?

00:01:56.397 --> 00:01:57.409
Pause to work it out.

00:02:04.339 --> 00:02:13.408
The result is 225 dollars gross, 30 dollars
and 40 cents in listed deductions, and 194
dollars and 60 cents net.

00:02:13.638 --> 00:02:18.686
A common misconception is that the deposit
amount is gross pay; it is net in this
example.

00:02:18.916 --> 00:02:22.362
Another is that matching arithmetic proves a
pay statement is correct.

00:02:22.592 --> 00:02:24.471
It proves only that these lines add up.

00:02:24.701 --> 00:02:32.366
For a real discrepancy, compare the same pay
dates with time records and the agreement,
then ask payroll or check current official
guidance.

00:02:32.596 --> 00:02:35.762
Never use this classroom example as a real
deduction rate.

00:02:35.992 --> 00:02:40.443
Suppose the time record showed 11.5 hours
instead of 12.5.

00:02:40.673 --> 00:02:50.259
At the fictional rate, gross would be 11.5
times 18 dollars, or 207 dollars, which is 18
dollars less than the printed 225.

00:02:50.489 --> 00:02:56.085
That difference is a question to check against
the same dates and actual work record, not
proof of intent.

00:02:56.315 --> 00:03:01.572
A clear follow-up would identify the exact pay
period and ask payroll to review the hour
entry.

00:03:01.802 --> 00:03:05.517
The invented deduction lines should not be
scaled to estimate a real cheque.

